---
import BaseLayout from '../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
const faqSchema = {
 "@context": "https://schema.org",
 "@type": "FAQPage",
 "mainEntity": [
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Is SMTP only for sending?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: SMTP handles outgoing mail and server-to-server relay. Receiving uses IMAP or POP3 on the client side and delivery into the mailbox store on the server side."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Why is port 25 blocked on my ISP?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "ISPs block outbound port 25 on consumer lines to stop spam botnets. Use your ISP's smarthost or a provider's submission port instead."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Should I use 465 or 587?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Either: 465 is implicit TLS from the first byte, 587 starts plaintext and upgrades via STARTTLS. Modern guidance accepts both; 587 is the traditional submission port."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Does IMAP need SMTP?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Yes: IMAP only stores and syncs. Sending always rides SMTP."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "What is Exchange ActiveSync then?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "A Microsoft sync protocol that handles mail, calendar, and contacts together; conceptually the IMAP+SMTP replacement in Microsoft ecosystems."
   }
  },
  {
   "@type": "Question",
   "name": "Can I use IMAP without TLS?",
   "acceptedAnswer": {
    "@type": "Answer",
    "text": "Technically yes on port 143, but you should never: credentials cross the network in plaintext. Always 993 with TLS."
   }
  }
 ]
}
---
<BaseLayout
  title="IMAP vs SMTP vs POP3: Email Protocols Explained | PromptDNS"
  description="IMAP vs SMTP vs POP3 explained: which protocol receives, which sends, how they work together, and which to choose for your devices."
  schema={[faqSchema]}
>
  <div class="mx-auto max-w-3xl px-4 py-12">
    <nav class="mb-4 text-sm text-ink-faint" aria-label="Breadcrumb">
      <a href="/" class="hover:text-accent">Home</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <a href="/email-authentication/" class="hover:text-accent">Email Authentication</a>
       <span aria-hidden="true">/</span>
      <span class="text-ink-dim">IMAP vs SMTP</span>
    </nav>
    <h1 class="font-display text-3xl font-bold tracking-tight text-ink sm:text-4xl">IMAP vs SMTP vs POP3: Email Protocols Explained</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Every email you send or receive uses at least two protocols: one to move mail between servers, one to sync it to your devices. SMTP sends; IMAP and POP3 receive. The differences decide how your mail behaves across phone, laptop, and webmail.</p>
    <div class="rich-text mt-8">
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">The division of labor</h2>
      <div class="overflow-x-auto rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60"><table class="pdns-table"><thead><tr><th>Protocol</th><th>Job</th><th>Ports</th><th>Behavior</th></tr></thead><tbody><tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-accent">SMTP</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Sending and server-to-server relay</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">587 (STARTTLS), 465 (TLS), 25 (server relay)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Push only: outgoing</td></tr><tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-accent">IMAP</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiving + multi-device sync</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">993 (TLS)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Mail stays on server; all devices mirror</td></tr><tr><td class="font-mono text-xs text-accent">POP3</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Receiving to one device</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">995 (TLS)</td><td class="text-sm text-ink-dim">Downloads and (usually) deletes from server</td></tr></tbody></table></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">IMAP vs POP3: the real decision</h2>
      <p>IMAP keeps mail on the server: read on your phone, it shows as read on your laptop. POP3 downloads to one device and typically removes it from the server: fine for a single-desktop archive, poor for modern multi-device life. Default to IMAP unless you specifically want server-side deletion.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">How they cooperate</h2>
      <p>You compose in your client, the client hands it to your SMTP submission server (587/465 with authentication), that server relays via SMTP port 25 to the recipient’s MX host, and the recipient’s IMAP/POP3 service delivers it to their devices. Both ends, four protocol touches.</p>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Frequently asked questions</h2>
      <div class="mt-3 grid gap-3"><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Is SMTP only for sending?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: SMTP handles outgoing mail and server-to-server relay. Receiving uses IMAP or POP3 on the client side and delivery into the mailbox store on the server side.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Why is port 25 blocked on my ISP?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">ISPs block outbound port 25 on consumer lines to stop spam botnets. Use your ISP's smarthost or a provider's submission port instead.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Should I use 465 or 587?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Either: 465 is implicit TLS from the first byte, 587 starts plaintext and upgrades via STARTTLS. Modern guidance accepts both; 587 is the traditional submission port.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Does IMAP need SMTP?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Yes: IMAP only stores and syncs. Sending always rides SMTP.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">What is Exchange ActiveSync then?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">A Microsoft sync protocol that handles mail, calendar, and contacts together; conceptually the IMAP+SMTP replacement in Microsoft ecosystems.</p></details><details class="group rounded-xl border border-line bg-panel/60 px-5 py-4 open:border-accent/40"><summary class="flex cursor-pointer list-none items-center justify-between gap-4 font-semibold text-ink [&::-webkit-details-marker]:hidden">Can I use IMAP without TLS?<span class="text-accent transition-transform duration-200 group-open:rotate-45">+</span></summary><p class="mt-3 text-sm leading-relaxed text-ink-dim">Technically yes on port 143, but you should never: credentials cross the network in plaintext. Always 993 with TLS.</p></details></div>
      <h2 class="font-display mt-10 text-xl font-semibold text-ink">Related</h2>
      <ul class="mt-3 grid gap-2"><li><a href="/smtp-tester/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Email ports explained →</a></li><li><a href="/email-authentication/" class="text-accent hover:text-accent-soft">Email authentication hub →</a></li></ul>
    </div>
  </div>
</BaseLayout>
